From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU1 interpreter: stores and VI writes at issue for translated code, flags applied on demand

What it is for:
  The per-cycle commit pass ran on almost every VU cycle of translated
  code: every FMAC instruction queued a flag entry, and stores and VI
  writes were queued to be committed one cycle later.

The change:
  - Flags (FMAC MAC/status, CLIP, FSSET, FCSET): every entry is due
    kFmacLatency cycles after issue and within a cycle the upper entry comes
    first, so the flag pipeline is a queue in issue order. It is now a
    16-entry ring (appendFlagEntry) that the commit pass no longer visits:
    materializeFlags() applies the due entries, in issue order, when the
    flags are read (the ten flag-reading lower instructions), before the
    FDIV status update (which came after the flags in the same commit
    pass), at the end of run(), and when the ring is full. Entries not yet
    due count as pending, as before, so the end-of-program flush takes the
    same cycles.
  - Stores from translated code (lower executor with a known word) are
    written at once (storeOrQueue): a store is due one cycle after issue,
    and that cycle's commit runs at the end of the same pair's step, before
    PATH1 reads VU memory, with nothing in between that reads memory. The
    interpreter keeps queueing them.
  - VI writes with latency 1 from translated code are written at once (as
    the commit would, 16-bit) and become the register's latest write, so an
    older queued write (ILW, latency 4) is dropped as before. Writes with a
    longer latency stay queued.
  Same results. Tested: ps2xTest 454/454; the Ben 10 port's 40 captured
  VU1 workloads give the same checksums and cycle count with and without
  its translated code; translated code 321 M -> 291 M instructions.

